WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.425
An AI homelab produces and consumes many kinds of data: prompts, outputs, datasets,

00:00:05.425 --> 00:00:10.400
documents, generated files, embeddings, model artifacts, logs, screenshots, notebooks,

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.540
and configuration.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.640
Some of that data may be sensitive even if the lab is personal.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.040
Prompts can include names, internal notes, source code, personal records,

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.680
or client information.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.000
Outputs can repeat or transform that information.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.287
Logs can capture paths, tokens, errors, and identifiers.

00:00:25.287 --> 00:00:26.360
Organize data intentionally.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:30.070
Separate personal data, client data, experiments, public test data,

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:31.620
and stable project assets.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.300
Use clear folders for models, datasets, outputs, temporary processing,

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.480
and archived results.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.760
This makes backup and deletion decisions easier.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.885
It also reduces the chance that a public demo, shared screenshot, cloud sync folder,

00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:47.115
or repository commit accidentally includes information that should have stayed private.

00:00:47.115 --> 00:00:50.695
Backups should focus on the assets that are hard to recreate: configuration, notes,

00:00:50.695 --> 00:00:54.145
prompts you need to preserve, curated datasets, application code,

00:00:54.145 --> 00:00:55.555
and workflow definitions.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:57.800
Large model files can often be downloaded again,

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.860
while carefully prepared datasets or notebooks may be unique.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.300
Encryption at rest may be appropriate when sensitive information is stored.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.175
Retention and deletion matter too.

00:01:06.175 --> 00:01:10.560
Keeping every experiment forever can turn a learning lab into a growing privacy liability.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.560
Treat the homelab as a data-handling environment.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.680
Decide what data is allowed, where it can be stored, whether cloud sync is acceptable,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.135
who can access shared folders, and how outputs are reviewed before sharing.

00:01:20.135 --> 00:01:23.660
Avoid putting secrets in prompts, logs, notebooks, or screenshots.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:27.440
Local AI can improve privacy compared with sending everything to a cloud service,

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.720
but only if the local environment is managed carefully.
